      "content": "Ah, the \"Goblin Reorder Point\" is a term I've been using in my head for a long time, though it's not a standard industry term like \"Economic Order Quantity.\" It's a metaphorical concept I've developed based on how goblins in the deep mines of the Goblin Kingdom handle their own chaotic supply chains.\n\nIn the Goblin Kingdom, when a goblin needs a specific type of glowing red gemstone, they don't wait for the merchant to arrive. Instead, they set a threshold: if the pile of gems on their shelf drops below the size of a single, large, angry goblin, they immediately send a messenger. Why? Because goblins are notoriously bad at predicting when their friends will show up, and waiting too long usually results in the merchant getting eaten by a dragon or simply forgetting the deal entirely.\n\nSo, the \"Goblin Reorder Point\" is simply your current inventory level dropping below a specific, low threshold that accounts for the worst-case scenario of a supplier disappearing or getting delayed by a sudden goblin uprising. It's a heuristic that prioritizes panic-prevention over mathematical perfection.\n\nTo finish the breakdown I started earlier for your lithium-ion batteries:\n\n4. **Safety",
